<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="cola_bottle">
    <!-- Link origin sits at the center of the bottle's base, same convention
         as jack_daniels_bottle, so placing the model's pose z at table height
         puts the bottle exactly on the surface. -->
    <link name="cola_link">
      <inertial>
        <!-- A mostly-full bottle carries its mass low; the liquid in the mesh
             tops out around z=0.227, so the centre of mass sits near 0.11.
             bartender_pour grasps at 0.060 (the waist) rather than at the
             centre of mass, so unlike the whiskey this one is not balanced in
             the fingers - see the COLA notes in pour_action_server.py.

             1.05kg: a refillable 500ml glass Coke bottle is ~550g of glass
             (they are made thick to survive being washed and reused) plus
             500g of contents. It was 0.8kg. Same reasoning as the whiskey -
             see that file's inertial comment for why mass helps here and what
             it does not do. Inertia is the solid-cylinder proxy (r 0.0336,
             h 0.250) rescaled; recompute it alongside any mass change. -->
        <pose>0 0 0.11 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>1.05</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.005765</ixx>
          <iyy>0.005765</iyy>
          <izz>0.000593</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <!-- The cola spins on the counter and will not settle. Traced: after a
           reset its yaw runs 72 to 141 degrees at a steady 2.3 deg/s with no
           sign of decay, and its tilt creeps to 4.6 degrees with it. That is
           enough to move the NECK 18mm sideways at grasp height, which is 30x
           the pocket clearance the neck-hooking fingertip has, so the grasp
           simply misses.

           The whiskey does not do this because its base is a BOX and gets a
           wide contact manifold; this base is a cylinder on a flat counter,
           which gives a narrow one that barely resists spin. Damping is the
           honest fix: a real bottle on a real bar does not rotate forever. -->
      <velocity_decay>
        <linear>0.05</linear>
        <angular>0.30</angular>
      </velocity_decay>

      <visual name="cola_visual">
        <!-- Source mesh is Y-up (Blender OBJ export), so rotate +90 deg about
             X to stand it up in Z, and scale it: the supplied mesh is 3.8418
             units tall, and 0.065074 makes that a 0.25m, 67.5mm-wide bottle,
             which is a 500ml glass Coke bottle. -->
        <pose>0 0 0 1.570796 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/cola_bottle.obj</uri>
            <scale>0.065074 0.065074 0.065074</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.06 0.02 0.01 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.28 0.08 0.05 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.5 0.5 0.5 1</specular>
          <emissive>0 0 0 1</emissive>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Primitive collisions rather than the 52k-face visual mesh. Profiling
           the mesh by height gives the classic contour-bottle shape:

             z 0.000..0.040   base, 65-67mm
             z 0.040..0.080   the WAIST, pinching to 57mm
             z 0.080..0.150   upper bulge, back out to 67.5mm
             z 0.150..0.195   shoulder, tapering 60mm -> 42mm
             z 0.195..0.250   neck and cap, 29-33mm

           The waist is modelled rather than smoothed away, and bartender_pour
           grips there, because the contour is what makes this bottle holdable.
           A uniform cylinder at the bottle's widest was tried first and the
           grasp was unreliable in a way no clamp angle fixed: a smooth
           cylinder between two flat pads needs ~6mm of pad penetration before
           the joint stalls (the whiskey's flat faces stall in 0.2mm), and at
           that depth the bottle rolls out sideways: traced, it spun 111
           degrees and was flung off the counter. Squeezing less made it slip
           on the lift instead.

           Seated in the waist the pads have 13mm of clearance a side instead
           of 9mm, and the 67mm sections above and below act as stops: the
           bottle can shift about 6mm before a wider part runs into pads that
           are closed to 59mm and cannot pass it. That is a geometric limit on
           the slip rather than a frictional one. Round, so nothing here
           depends on the bottle's yaw. -->
      <collision name="cola_base_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.0200 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.0333</radius>
            <length>0.040</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.9</mu>
              <mu2>0.9</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <collision name="cola_waist_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.0600 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.0298</radius>
            <length>0.040</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.9</mu>
              <mu2>0.9</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <collision name="cola_bulge_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.1150 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.0336</radius>
            <length>0.070</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.9</mu>
              <mu2>0.9</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <collision name="cola_shoulder_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.1725 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.0250</radius>
            <length>0.045</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.9</mu>
              <mu2>0.9</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <collision name="cola_neck_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.2225 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.0165</radius>
            <length>0.055</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.9</mu>
              <mu2>0.9</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <!-- Collar ring, for picking the bottle up BY THE NECK. The fingertip
           in ../../fingertip hooks its ledge under this, so the bottle hangs
           on a mechanical ledge instead of on pad friction.

           Real spirits bottles have this lip; the model did not, and without
           it a neck hook cannot work at all: going up the bottle the
           profile only ever narrowed, so there was nothing above the neck for
           a ledge to catch. r 0.0195 against the 0.0165 neck gives a 3mm
           radial overhang, which is what the ledge bears on. Sits just below
           the mouth, leaving 5mm of lip above and the neck bare below. -->
      <collision name="cola_collar_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.2410 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.0210</radius>
            <length>0.008</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.9</mu>
              <mu2>0.9</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <!-- ============ Speed pourer ============
           A bar speed pourer (the tapered spout you hammer into a spirits
           bottle) rather than pouring from the bare lip. It does two things
           that matter here.

           It makes the pour controlled instead of a glug: a bare mouth
           alternates liquid and air and comes out in surges, which is why
           real bars fit these.

           And, more importantly for the robot, it moves the POUR POINT off
           the bottle's axis. The bare mouth sat on the axis, so every pour
           pose could be solved from the bottle's centreline. The spout tip
           is 17.1mm to the side and 54mm further along, and that
           point - not the mouth, not tool0 - is what has to be held over
           the glass. That is what the tool frames in
           bartender_teach/tool_frames.py exist to express: with the bottle
           grasped, TIP is the tool centre point, and a tilt rotates ABOUT it
           so it keeps its xyz while the bottle turns around it.

           Tip in bottle-local coordinates: (0.0171, 0, 0.3040).
           It leans toward local +X on purpose. The pour tilts the bottle
           about base Y, which maps bottle-local +X onto base -Z at the pour
           angle - so leaning that way is what points the spout DOWNWARD
           when the bottle is tilted, instead of uselessly sideways.

           Mass is left alone: a pourer is ~20g against a 1.05kg bottle,
           which is inside the rounding on that figure, and it raises the
           centre of mass by about 2mm. -->
      <visual name="cola_pourer_body_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0.2460 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.0095</radius><length>0.022</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.05 0.05 0.06 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.22 0.23 0.26 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="cola_pourer_spout_visual">
        <pose>0.0086 0 0.2805 0 0.349066 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.005</radius><length>0.050</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.05 0.05 0.06 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.22 0.23 0.26 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <collision name="cola_pourer_body_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.2460 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.0095</radius><length>0.022</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction></surface>
      </collision>
      <collision name="cola_pourer_spout_collision">
        <pose>0.0086 0 0.2805 0 0.349066 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.005</radius><length>0.050</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction></surface>
      </collision>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
